## Your rights at work

Cyclothymia can count as a disability under the **Equality Act 2010** if it has a **substantial, long-term effect** on your daily life. Your employer must consider **reasonable adjustments**, such as:

- **flexible hours** during low periods
- **regular sleep-friendly shifts**
- time off for **appointments**
- regular **check-ins** with your manager

**Access to Work** offers a free **Mental Health Support Service**.

## Benefits

PIP is based on how your condition affects you on **most days**. Many people with cyclothymia will not qualify on this condition alone, but if your moods regularly stop you managing daily life, for example if you need **prompting** to wash, eat or cook during lows, or **help with money** because of impulsive spending during highs, it may be worth checking.

{% checker tool="pip" /%}

If you cannot work because of your mental health, you may be able to get **[Universal Credit](/benefits/universal-credit-health/)** or **[New Style Employment and Support Allowance](/benefits/esa/)**.
